WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.780
Threat hunters rely on many sources of information.

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:07.390
These may include EDR telemetry, SIEM logs, authentication events, DNS activity,

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:11.860
firewall records, cloud logs, proxy logs, endpoint artifacts, network telemetry,

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.120
and threat intelligence.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.955
The quality of a hunt often depends on the quality and completeness of available data.

00:00:16.955 --> 00:00:18.955
Missing visibility creates blind spots.

00:00:18.955 --> 00:00:23.400
A hypothesis cannot be tested reliably if the organization does not collect the telemetry

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.050
needed to observe the behavior.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.320
Context also matters.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.390
An unusual process on one system may be normal on another.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:33.320
A login pattern may be expected for one role but suspicious for a different user,

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.740
location, time, or system.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.310
Hunters combine technical evidence with environmental knowledge to make informed

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:38.890
judgments.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.355
They look for patterns, timelines, relationships,

00:00:41.355 --> 00:00:45.305
and exceptions that help distinguish normal operations from activity that needs further

00:00:45.305 --> 00:00:45.920
investigation.
